Message catalog maintenance
===========================

Meson provides init-po and update-po, and <catalog>-init-po and
<catalog>-update-po for each configured catalog.  Outputs live in the
component's po directory in the build tree.  xgettext and msgmerge are
maintenance tools; ordinary builds do not require them.  Runtime NLS need
not be enabled.  Enable optional procedural languages explicitly when
preparing catalogs for Babel.

Both .pot and .po.new files are declared custom-target outputs.  An unchanged
invocation does no work.  Changes to sources, generator inputs, extraction
commands, original translations or same-language compendia rebuild the
affected outputs.  Clean removes the outputs.  Configure -Dpo_languages=de,fr
to restrict merging; the empty default includes all languages listed in the
source LINGUAS files.  Unknown codes are ignored.  LINGUAS changes trigger
reconfiguration.  Compendia include disabled optional components too.

Catalog registrations
---------------------

Each component registers a dictionary in po_catalogs, keyed by its
source-root-relative directory.  name is the unversioned catalog name;
sources contains ordinary Meson file objects; generated contains producer
targets for generated extraction inputs.  frontend and
backend select common keyword and format-flag sets.  keywords and flags add
component-specific settings.  The po/meson.build file declares outputs in
the appropriate directory and retains the existing i18n.gettext installation
rules and versioned text domains.

Reuse ordinary compilation source lists before adding resource files.  For
conditional sources, declare the collections before selecting the compilation
variant and include all variants in extraction.  Do not use directory scans:
they miss generated inputs in fresh builds and can pick up stale files.
Message-bearing headers and included C fragments need explicit entries.
Shared sources belong in each catalog that translates them at runtime;
linking libpq does not make its messages part of the caller's text domain.

Generated inputs
----------------

Pass generators in generated, not filenames guessed in the build tree.
This includes GUC tables, psql SQL help and tab completion, and PL/Perl SPI.c.
Extract Bison grammars before Bison adds its implementation: ordinary .y files
are inputs directly, while ECPG's preproc.y is a generated target.

Flex syntax is not C and feeding raw .l files to xgettext produces lexical
warnings.  Instead, scanners are generated normally, and maintain_po.py keeps
only regions attributed to .l inputs by Flex's #line directives.  This retains
PostgreSQL's scanner actions and comments without extracting Flex scaffolding.

The helper stages inputs in a temporary source-shaped tree, normalizes #line
paths and invokes xgettext using catalog-relative filenames.  Line numbers
are preserved; compiler inputs are never modified.  Files without source
mappings, such as sql_help.c and guc_tables.inc.c, retain stable logical
generated-file references.  Normalization precedes sorting, making reference
spelling and order independent of the build location.  Each output replaces
its predecessor only after gettext succeeds.

Make and PGXS
-------------

In-tree Makefiles set CATALOG_NAME before including Makefile.global for
compiling and installing existing translations.  In-tree extraction uses
Meson.  External PGXS extensions can still provide nls.mk.  nls-global.mk retains
legacy extraction settings and targets for external PGXS extensions; it does
not need Python, JSON metadata or an adapter.  Older branches, particularly
14 and 15, continue to use their existing Make maintenance workflow.

Shared translations
-------------------

Sharing extraction inputs and compendia preserves existing text domains but
does not eliminate repeated translation maintenance.  A follow-up can give
shared components translator-facing catalogs and compose program catalogs in
the build tree before msgfmt.  That requires coordinated Babel publication,
seeding translations and resolving conflicts, defining precedence and plural
headers, and providing composition in both Make and Meson.  It must also
handle missing languages, fuzzy translations, and context-specific overrides.

Verification
------------

With xgettext and msgmerge installed, meson test --suite po exercises scanner
filtering, source references across build locations, plurals, translator
comments, compendium initialization, manifests, and failed-output preservation.
The tests do not need a database server.

When changing source selection, compare every catalog with a fully generated
Make baseline, not a VPATH backend scan that omits generated files.  Compare
message IDs, plural forms, format flags and translator comments separately
from references and creation dates.  Reusing the complete compilation lists
also extracts messages previously omitted from libpq's fe-print.c, ECPG's
parser.c and the LLVM C++ sources.

For build-graph changes, test a fresh init-po, a repeated no-op invocation,
changes to ordinary and generated inputs, a same-language compendium edit,
and source additions/removals in the compilation list.  Compare catalogs in
different build locations with SSL, GSSAPI and OAuth disabled as well as
enabled.  Test optional languages separately, and verify that clean removes
the declared outputs.  Make all-po and an installed PGXS extension's
init-po/update-po/all-po cover the compatibility boundary.
